'''Ryan Michael Fazio'''<ref name="United States Public Records">United States Public Records</ref> (born March 27, 1990) is an American businessman and politician who is a member of the [[Connecticut State Senate]] for the [[Connecticut's 36th State Senate district|36th district]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Meet Ryan |url=https://www.ryanfazio.com/meet-ryan |access-date=2023-02-19 |website=Ryan Fazio |language=en}}</ref> A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he won election in 2021 following the resignation of [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] incumbent [[Alexandra Kasser]], and was re-elected in 2022 and 2024.

He is the Republican nominee for [[governor of Connecticut]] in the [[2026 Connecticut gubernatorial election|2026 election]] and, if elected, would become the first governor in U.S. history to have been born in the 1990s.

== Early life and education ==
Fazio was born March 27, 1990 in [[Norwalk, Connecticut]], the oldest of three children, to Michael Fazio, a former partner of [[Arthur Andersen]] and managing director of [[Houlihan Lokey]], and Madeline Fazio ([[Birth name|née]] Gadaleta).<ref>{{Cite web |title=From Pace to Texas |url=https://www.alumni.pace.edu/s/1655/bp20/interior.aspx?sid=1655&gid=2&pgid=2766&cid=6131&ecid=6131&crid=0&calpgid=13&calcid=664 |access-date=2023-02-19 |website=www.alumni.pace.edu |language=en}}</ref> 

He has two brothers, Michael Fazio and Christian Fazio, as well as one half-sister Anastasia Fazio.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kaufman |first=Richard |last2=Staff |first2=Patch |date=2024-10-16 |title=CT Patch Candidate Profile: Ryan Fazio For State Senator |url=https://patch.com/connecticut/greenwich/ct-patch-candidate-profile-ryan-fazio-state-senator |access-date=2026-10-14 |website=Greenwich, CT Patch |language=en}}</ref> He grew up in [[Greenwich, Connecticut]], attending the local public schools, and subsequently earned a Bachelor of Arts in Economics from Northwestern University in 2012.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Ryan Fazio|url=https://ballotpedia.org/Ryan_Fazio|access-date=2021-08-18|website=Ballotpedia|language=en}}</ref>

== Professional career ==
Fazio spent most of his career in the [[commodities]] industry, with a focus on renewable fuels and agriculture. He is currently a [[financial advisor]].<ref>{{cite-web|title= Who is Republican Ryan Fazio, a candidate for Connecticut governor?|url= https://www.newstimes.com/politics/article/ct-governor-race-2026-ryan-fazio-21316482.php?|website=News Times|access-date=May 18, 2026}}</ref>

== Political career ==
=== State Senate ===
Before being elected to the state Senate, Fazio was elected to the [[Greenwich, Connecticut|Greenwich]] [[representative town meeting]].<ref>{{Cite web|author=Ken Borsuk|url=https://www.greenwichtime.com/elections/article/Republican-Fazio-says-he-is-a-proponent-for-16355118.php|title=Republican Fazio says he is a 'proponent for reform' in his second run for state Senate|work=Greenwich Time|date=August 1, 2021}}</ref> On August 17, 2021, Fazio defeated Democratic lawyer Alexis Gevanter of [[Greenwich, Connecticut|Greenwich]] in a special election to fill the [[Connecticut's 36th State Senate district|36th district]] seat, which [[Alexandra Kasser]]<ref name="se">{{Cite web|last=Altimari|first=Jessika Harkay, Daniela|title=Fazio wins special election to return Fairfield County Senate seat to Republicans in special election|url=https://www.courant.com/breaking-news/hc-br-greenwich-farifield-county-senate-election-20210818-za4o6owwerbl7e6owkoef6yw7q-story.html|access-date=2021-08-18|website=courant.com|date=2021-08-18}}</ref> vacated two months prior.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Altimari|first=Daniela|title=State Sen. Alex Kasser resigns and says she is leaving Greenwich because of stress from long-running divorce case|url=https://www.courant.com/politics/hc-pol-alex-kasser-senator-resign-20210622-6peeld22wjaelitmjb6fjwtajy-story.html|access-date=2021-08-18|website=courant.com|date=2021-06-22}}</ref> His election ended the Democratic [[supermajority]] in the State Senate.<ref name="se"/> He was re-elected in 2022 against Democrat Trevor Crow and in 2024 against Democrat [[Nick Simmons (politician)|Nick Simmons]], former deputy chief of staff to Governor [[Ned Lamont]]. Fazio’s State Senate races have been noted as some of the most spent State Senate races in the history of [[Connecticut]]. He was the Republican nominee for the same seat in the [[2020 Connecticut State Senate election]]. Connecticut's 36th district covers [[Greenwich, Connecticut|Greenwich]] and parts of [[Stamford, Connecticut|Stamford]] and [[New Canaan]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Borsuk|first=Ken|date=2021-08-18|title=Republican Fazio declares victory in race for 36th State Senate District|url=https://www.greenwichtime.com/elections/article/Republican-Fazio-declares-victory-in-race-for-16394045.php|access-date=2021-08-18|website=GreenwichTime|language=en-US}}</ref>

=== 2026 Connecticut gubernatorial election ===
{{Main|2026 Connecticut gubernatorial election}}

In 2026, Fazio became the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominee for governor of Connecticut in 2026. He announced his candidacy in 2025. At the beginning of the campaign, former New Britain Mayor [[Erin Stewart]] was seen as the front runner in a tight race with Fazio. Stewart later suspended her campaign and endorsed Fazio a day before the convention. At the Connecticut Republican State Convention on May 16, 2026, at the [[Mohegan Sun|Mohegan Sun Casino]] in [[Uncasville, Connecticut|Uncasville]], he won the party’s endorsement on the first ballot with about 92% of the delegate vote. His remaining opponent, [[Betsy McCaughey]], conceded shortly after at the convention.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dixon |first=Ken |date=2026-05-16 |title=Connecticut Republicans nominate Ryan Fazio for governor after Erin Stewart drops out of race |url=https://www.ctinsider.com/politics/article/ct-republican-convention-nominations-5-16-26-fazio-22224016.php |access-date=2026-05-16 |website=CT Insider |language=en}}</ref> Fazio crossed the $250,000 threshold necessary to qualify for Connecticut's public financing system at the fastest rate in state history.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pazniokas |first=Mark |date=2026-01-12 |title=GOP's Ryan Fazio reaches public financing goal in governor race |url=https://ctmirror.org/2026/01/12/ryan-fazio-governor-public-financing/ |access-date=2026-01-28 |website=CT Mirror |language=en-US}}</ref>

Fazio will run with lieutenant governor candidate Matthew Corey, a small businessman and Navy veteran. He will face Democratic incumbent governor [[Ned Lamont]] in the general election on November 3, 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dixon |first=Ken |date=2026-05-16 |title=Connecticut Republicans nominate Ryan Fazio for governor after Erin Stewart drops out of race |url=https://www.ctinsider.com/politics/article/ct-republican-convention-nominations-5-16-26-fazio-22224016.php |access-date=2026-05-16 |website=CT Insider |language=en}}</ref>

== Political positions ==

=== Energy ===
As ranking senator (the second highest ranked senator on the committee, behind co-Chair [[Norman Needleman|Norm Needleman]]) of the state’s Energy & Technology Committee, Fazio has been a voice on energy issues and critic of current state policies. In 2023, he co-authored parts of that year's SB7, including a section requiring utilities to itemize the cost of dozens of energy and environmental programs run by the state government on residents’ monthly bills.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Vecchitto |first=Jack |date=2023-05-26 |title=(Photo) Senator Fazio Commends Senate on Passage of Bipartisan Legislation Aimed to Decrease Consumer Energy Rates |url=https://www.ctsenaterepublicans.com/2023/05/photo-senator-fazio-commends-senate-on-passage-of-bipartisan-legislation-aimed-to-decrease-consumer-energy-rates/ |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=Connecticut Senate Republicans |language=en-US}}</ref> That category, called "Public Benefits", has become a source of debate. It includes subsidies for solar power, electric vehicle chargers, low-income households, and many other programs.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Fitch |first=Marc E. |date=2025-04-28 |title=CT ratepayers paying over $1 billion per year in public benefits charges |url=https://insideinvestigator.org/ct-ratepayers-paying-over-1-billion-per-year-in-public-benefits-charges/ |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=Connecticut Inside Investigator |language=en-US}}</ref>

Fazio has repeatedly proposed legislation to eliminate "Public Benefits" charges from residents' energy bills, either eliminating programs or funding them in the state budget process.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Vecchitto |first=Jack |date=2025-04-16 |title=Sen. Fazio & Harding Statement on CT Energy Legislation |url=https://www.ctsenaterepublicans.com/2025/04/sen-fazio-harding-statement-on-ct-energy-legislation/ |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=Connecticut Senate Republicans |language=en-US}}</ref> Fazio's proposals have drawn disagreement from progressive environmental groups and support among Republican legislators. In 2025, Fazio subsequently co-authored that year's SB4, which cut over $100 million per year from "Public Benefits" charges, although it was a compromise and didn't eliminate roughly $1 billion in costs as Fazio originally proposed.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bowe |first=Marvin |date=2025-06-03 |title=CT Senate GOP Celebrates Affordability Relief: S.B. 4 to Reduce Electric Bills by Millions |url=https://www.ctsenaterepublicans.com/2025/06/ct-senate-gop-celebrates-affordability-relief-s-b-4-to-reduce-electric-bills-by-millions/ |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=Connecticut Senate Republicans |language=en-US}}</ref> Gov. Ned Lamont gave Fazio some "credit" for advancing the law.<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eBfqGFRSyis |title=News conference to discuss the adjournment of the 2025 Legislative Session |date=2025-06-06 |last=Governor Ned Lamont |access-date=2025-08-13 |via=YouTube}}</ref>

Fazio has also been a supporter of expanding natural gas pipeline capacity from the Marcellus shale region and better regulatory treatment for nuclear power.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bowe |first=Marvin |date=2025-02-20 |title=Statement from Sen. Fazio on New York Governor Boosting Natural Gas Supply and Pipeline Capacity |url=https://www.ctsenaterepublicans.com/2025/02/statement-from-sen-fazio-on-new-york-governor-boosting-natural-gas-supply-and-pipeline-capacity/ |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=Connecticut Senate Republicans |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Taxes and spending ===
Fazio is a fiscal conservative who has supported tax cuts and opposed large spending increases over his tenure. He earned a career 100% rating from the Connecticut Business and Industry Association for his voting record in support of "economic growth".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Budd |first=Joe |date=2024-07-17 |title=2024 State Senate Voting Records » CBIA |url=https://www.cbia.com/resources/issues-policies/2024-state-senate-voting-records/ |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=CBIA |language=en-US}}</ref>

Fazio introduced a tax plan, [https://www.cga.ct.gov/asp/cgabillstatus/cgabillstatus.asp?selBillType=Bill&which_year=2025&bill_num=602 SB602], calling for a 1.5 percentage-point income tax cut for middle-income households. It would fund the tax cut by reducing tax expenditures and state bonding. He also introduced a proposal for a property tax cap, [https://www.cga.ct.gov/2025/TOB/S/PDF/2025SB-00556-R00-SB.PDF SB556], that would cap increases in property taxes to 2 percent or inflation every year, like the law in Massachusetts.

Fazio was a vocal proponent of the state's so-called "fiscal guardrails", which were passed in 2017 and included a spending cap, "volatility cap", bonding cap, and revenue cap with the goal of mitigating the state’s tax increases and debt overhang.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Benjamin |first=Scott |date=2025-01-04 |title=Preserving the guard rails is a 'top priority' |url=https://patch.com/connecticut/brookfield/preserving-guard-rails-top-priority |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=Brookfield, CT Patch |language=en}}</ref>

=== Abortion ===
Fazio has stated he is in favor of keeping abortion legal in Connecticut.<ref>{{Cite web |title=CT Patch Candidate Profile: Ryan Fazio For 36th State Senate District |date=October 31, 2022|url=https://patch.com/connecticut/greenwich/ct-patch-candidate-profile-ryan-fazio-36th-state-senate-district |website=Patch}}</ref> In 2022, Fazio voted against [[House Bill 5414]], which allows aspiration abortions to be performed by midwives or nurses without a doctor. He stated that it would lower the standard of care for women.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Vote for HB-5414 Roll Call Vote |url=https://www.cga.ct.gov/2022/VOTE/S/PDF/2022SV-00267-R00HB05414-SV.PDF |website=[[Connecticut General Assembly]]}}</ref>

In 2023, Fazio, along with the majority the Republican Senate conference, voted in favor of House Bill 6820, which increases public access to birth control in Connecticut, and also protects licensed medical providers from having their licenses revoked for providing an abortion.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Vote for HB-6820 Roll Call Vote |url=https://www.cga.ct.gov/2023/VOTE/S/PDF/2023SV-00423-R00HB06820-SV.PDF |website=[[Connecticut General Assembly]]}}</ref>

=== Contraceptives ===
In 2023, Fazio co-authored a bill to expand contraception access in Connecticut by allowing certain trained pharmacists to prescribe oral contraception. The proposal was included in HB6768 and passed into law.<ref>{{Cite web |title=An act concerning the department of consumer protection's recommendations regarding prescription drug regulation |url=https://cga.ct.gov/2023/lcoamd/pdf/2023LCO07022-R00-AMD.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230923104711/https://www.cga.ct.gov/2023/lcoamd/pdf/2023LCO07022-R00-AMD.pdf |archive-date=2023-09-23}}</ref>

=== Economic issues ===
Fazio wrote and moved legislation to cut occupational licensing fees for workers. Legislation that Fazio introduced with several other legislators would cut all fees for workers like tradesmen, nurses, accountants, and others, to a maximum of $100. The legislation passed the Senate in 2024, [https://www.cga.ct.gov/2024/lcoamd/pdf/2024LCO05802-R00-AMD.pdf SB135], but did not pass into law.{{citation needed|date=September 2025}}

Fazio passed a law in 2025, SB1558, aimed at bringing back hundreds of millions in income tax revenue collected by New York and other neighboring states from Connecticut residents while they work from home for companies based in the other states. The law offers a 60 percent credit to any resident successfully appealing or litigating against another state in this area. It also requires the Attorney General to write a legal strategy to challenge other states taking such tax revenue from Connecticut.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Craven |first=John |date=2025-06-24 |title=Work from home? A new state law could save you thousands of dollars in taxes |url=https://connecticut.news12.com/work-from-home-a-new-state-law-could-save-you-thousands-of-dollars-in-taxes |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=News 12 Connecticut}}</ref>

=== Legislation ===
Fazio has been able to author or co-author several new laws as a state senator despite Republicans’ minority-party status in the legislature:

* SB6768, Section 6, in 2023 to allow trained pharmacists to prescribe hormonal contraception.{{citation needed|date=September 2025}}
* SB7 in 2023 to reform oversight of utilities and require disclosure of "public benefits" charges on utility bills.{{citation needed|date=September 2025}}
* SB333 in 2024 to increase the ability of towns and cities to make changes to their municipal charters.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-17 |title=Passing SB 333 |url=https://www.newcanaansentinel.com/2024/05/17/passing-sb-333/ |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=New Canaan Sentinel |language=en-US}}</ref>
* A 2024 law to eliminate college degree requirements for most state jobs.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Recchia |first1=Frank |last2=Alarcon |first2=Nicole |date=2024-06-07 |title=Gov. Lamont signs new law dropping college degree requirements for state jobs |url=https://newjersey.news12.com/gov-lamont-signs-new-law-dropping-college-degree-requirements-for-state-jobs |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=News 12 Connecticut}}</ref>
* SB1558 in 2025 to prove financial support and state direction to sue New York and Massachusetts for taxing the income of Connecticut residents while they work from home for New York and Massachusetts companies.{{citation needed|date=September 2025}}
* SB4 in 2025 to cut "public benefits" costs, including cutting subsidies for EV charging stations, solar subsidies, and more.{{citation needed|date=September 2025}}

== Personal life ==
Fazio announced his engagement to Amy Elizabeth Orser (born 1997), a daughter of Henson Orser and Susan Orser ([[née]] Brink), of New Canaan, Connecticut, on August 10, 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |title=CAPITOL REPORT 2025® |url=https://www.ctcapitolreport.com/mondays/f7d38e3b32-coming-this-week-the-fazio-blitz |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=www.ctcapitolreport.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-08-21 |title=Engagement of Amy Orser and Ryan Fazio |url=https://www.newcanaansentinel.com/2025/08/21/engagement-of-amy-orser-and-ryan-fazio/ |access-date=2026-10-14 |website=New Canaan Sentinel |language=en-US}}</ref>

Fazio is a resident of the [[Riverside, Connecticut|Riverside section]] of [[Greenwich, Connecticut]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Overview of Mr. Ryan Michael Fazio |access-date=September 3, 2024 |url=https://voterrecords.com/voter/3729776/ryan-fazio |website=Voter Registration Records}}</ref> 

